        "text": "The trader begins to look for stocks they believe will rise in value within the trading day. They can base their decisions, perhaps on some expected news a company will release that day, the price of a stock at closing time the day before, or other information. For example, the day trader begins to buy Boeing and Coca Cola stock. Boeing is due to release details of the development of a new plane at midday and Coca Cola finished its trading day yesterday at an undervalued price after a big sell off. Over the day, both stocks begin to rise and the trader sells them for a profit. In our previous lessons, you'll remember that technical analysis is well suited to short term trading. Gains in such a short period can be minimal and sometimes don't just rely on the fundamental value of an asset. Day trading is a very active trading strategy that requires a significant time investment. It can be highly profitable, but it also has a substantial amount of risk. As such, day trading is generally better suited to experienced traders.",
        "start": 240,
